1
00:00:00,150 --> 00:00:03,278
Earth's magnetic poles have swapped places many times.

2
00:00:03,278 --> 00:00:05,248
What if it started again tomorrow?

3
00:00:05,750 --> 00:00:10,543
NASA counts a hundred and eighty-three flips in the last eighty-three million years.

4
00:00:10,543 --> 00:00:14,651
The last full one was about seven hundred and eighty thousand years ago.

5
00:00:15,150 --> 00:00:16,476
A flip isn't instant.

6
00:00:16,476 --> 00:00:19,001
It takes hundreds to thousands of years,

7
00:00:19,001 --> 00:00:21,842
and along the way the field gets much weaker,

8
00:00:21,842 --> 00:00:24,494
with extra poles popping up in odd places.

9
00:00:24,983 --> 00:00:26,474
Compasses get confused.

10
00:00:26,474 --> 00:00:28,482
Satellites take more radiation.

11
00:00:28,482 --> 00:00:31,398
And auroras could show up far from the poles.

12
00:00:31,883 --> 00:00:35,925
But the fossil record shows no mass extinctions caused by flips.

13
00:00:35,925 --> 00:00:38,703
Life has been through this many, many times.

14
00:00:39,217 --> 00:00:41,437
What if the field switched off completely?

15
00:00:41,437 --> 00:00:42,811
Full story on the channel.
